---
// Financing, starting where a lender starts: the credit file.
//
// Product comparisons date within a quarter and we would be inventing the
// numbers. The credit bureau's own mechanics do not date, are federal, and
// explain the thing founders are actually surprised by — that a company's
// thirty-six months of payment history is already on file before it ever
// applies for anything.
import VerticalLayout from "../../layouts/VerticalLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "@dst/ui/PageHero.astro";
import Callout from "@dst/ui/Callout.astro";
import Faq from "@dst/ui/Faq.astro";

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: "Does a Dubai company have a credit file?",
    a: "Yes. Al Etihad Credit Bureau, a public joint stock company wholly owned by the UAE federal government, issues credit reports for companies as well as individuals, nationals and residents alike.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is in it?",
    a: "A credit score, an income utilisation ratio, thirty-six months of payment history on loans and bills, a summary of credit contracts and cards, and court-ordered financial obligations. The data comes from banks, financial companies, telecom and utility providers, courts and government entities.",
  },
  {
    q: "How does a company get its own report?",
    a: "Apply on the AECB website, then attend any of the 250-plus Al Ansari Exchange branches in the UAE with the Emirates ID of a partner, shareholder or manager named on the trade licence, the application number, and a copy of the trade licence.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is that different from how an individual gets one?",
    a: "Yes, and it is the part that catches people out. An individual logs in with UAE Pass, pays online and receives the PDF instantly. A company's report has an in-person step, which means it has to be planned rather than produced in a meeting.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is Islamic finance available to a company?",
    a: "Yes. Financial products compliant with Islamic Shari'a banking guidelines are available alongside conventional ones, and the documents and procedures vary by bank because the products are backed by different assets.",
  },
];
---
<VerticalLayout
  vertical="llc"
  current="/llc/financing/"
  prose
  title="Financing a Dubai company: your credit file first"
  description="Before a Dubai bank prices anything it reads a federal credit file holding thirty-six months of payment history. How to get the company's own report, and why it takes a branch visit."
  image="/covers/llc-financing.jpg"
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Financing"
    title="Financing a Dubai company: your credit file first"
    image="/covers/llc-financing.jpg"
    imageAlt="Cut-paper illustration: a long strip of paper fanned into many narrow pleats, lying flat on a blue ground."
    imageKind="generated"
  >
    <p class="lede">
      Before any lender in the UAE quotes a company a number, it reads a file
      the company did not compile: a federal credit report holding
      <strong>thirty-six months</strong> of payment history, drawn from
      banks, finance companies, telecom and utility providers, courts and
      government entities. That file exists whether or not the company has
      ever borrowed. Reading it before the bank does is the cheapest
      preparation available.
    </p>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>Who holds it</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Al Etihad Credit Bureau</strong> — a public joint stock company
      wholly owned by the UAE federal government — issues credit reports for
      both companies and individuals. A report summarises personal
      information, financial obligations and bills for the past three years,
      and the last reported salary where there is one. Financial institutions
      pull it when assessing an application for a loan, a credit card or any
      other credit facility, which in practice means every conversation about
      money starts there.
    </p>
    <p>
      The report carries a <strong>credit score</strong>, an income
      utilisation ratio showing how payments weigh against reported income, a
      <strong>payment history</strong> covering the last thirty-six months, a
      summary of all credit contracts and cards, and a summary of
      court-ordered financial obligations.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting the company's own copy takes a branch visit</h2>
    <p>
      An individual logs in with UAE Pass, selects the report or the score,
      pays online and has the PDF instantly. A company does not. The
      application goes in on the AECB website, and then somebody attends one
      of the <strong>250-plus Al Ansari Exchange branches</strong> in the UAE
      carrying three things: the <strong>Emirates ID</strong> of a partner,
      shareholder or manager named on the trade licence, the
      <strong>application number</strong>, and a copy of the
      <strong>trade licence</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      That asymmetry is worth planning around. The company report cannot be
      produced in the meeting where it is first asked for, so it belongs on
      the list of things obtained before the funding conversation opens — next
      to the licence and the attested Memorandum of Association.
    </p>

    <h2>What the banks offer once they have read it</h2>
    <p>
      Financing options in the UAE vary by the company's activity, and the
      documents and procedures vary with them, because the products are
      backed by different assets. Products compliant with
      <strong>Islamic Shari'a banking guidelines</strong> are available
      alongside conventional ones. The published guidance for businesses
      running here reduces, in the end, to one instruction: settle payments
      on time to avoid penalties — which is also how the next thirty-six
      months of the file gets written.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Callout kind="warn">
    <p>
      A dispute that reaches court reaches the credit report. Court-ordered
      financial obligations are one of the report's five sections, which
      means an unpaid supplier invoice that turns litigious has a financing
      cost long after it has a legal one.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>Where this sits in the sequence</h2>
    <p>
      The licence comes first — see
      <a href="/llc/registration/" title="Registering a company in Dubai, step by step">registration</a> —
      then the account, whose terms are a matter of contract rather than
      consumer protection once the company is an LLC, on
      <a href="/llc/banking/" title="Opening a business account in Dubai">the banking page</a>.
      Financing comes third, and the tax position that shapes what the
      company can actually service is on
      <a href="/llc/taxation/" title="Corporate tax and VAT for a Dubai company">the tax page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqItems} />

  <section class="container">
    <p class="source-note">
      Credit report contents, the thirty-six-month history, AECB's ownership
      and the application routes for individuals and companies: the Official
      Platform of the UAE Government, credit report of individuals and
      companies. Financing options and Shari'a-compliant products: the same
      platform, running a business on the mainland. Both read 15 September
      2026. Nothing here is financial advice.
    </p>
  </section>
</VerticalLayout>
